  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, low stratus and persistent sky cover will\nremain entrenched across much of the Northland, except for areas of\nnorth-central Minnesota and inland northwest Wisconsin where some\nclearing will continue this afternoon. Dangerous rip currents will\ncontinue to impact the beaches of the Twin Ports into this evening.\nHeading into tonight, lingering moisture within northeast\nonshore flow is forecast to create dense fog along the South \nShore, where Dense Fog Advisories have been issued.\n\nSaturday will start off foggy along the South Shore and cloudy for\nmany, but conditions should gradually improve into the midday hours.\nA second day of dangerous rip currents is possible again \nSaturday for the beaches of the Twin Ports, and another Beach \nHazards Statement is likely to be needed. Temperatures will warm\nback into the upper 70s to low 80s as transient ridging builds \ninto the region.\n\nThe break in active weather ends very early Sunday morning as \nthunderstorms return then for the rest of the Holiday weekend. \nAs instability surges into the region, strong to severe \nthunderstorms are possible Sunday afternoon and evening. This \nenvironment will also be characterized by a very moist airmass, \nbringing the threat of heavy rainfall alongside any severe \nhazards. If it can form, low confidence right now, severe \nweather would mainly be limited to north- central Minnesota and \nthe Borderlands Sunday afternoon to evening before quickly \ndecreasing environmental favorability into northeast Minnesota.\n\nWidespread light to moderate wetting rainfall is forecast for Labor\nDay Monday, especially from the afternoon through Tuesday morning\nas a dynamic low pressure system approaches. This would be\nanother day of low-end severe weather chances for severe \nweather potentially in northern Minnesota, but much lesser \nfavorability into northwest Wisconsin.\n\nBy Tuesday afternoon though, the more likely severe weather\nchances for the region, cross over the entire Northland and\ncould be a day to watch given the more notable forcing combining\nwith a highly moist atmosphere. The instability though appears\nto be a narrow corridor that will have to line up with daytime\nheating to erode any mid-level capping.\n\nGale force wind gusts from easterly winds will impact the North\nShore on Monday, reaching up to 40 mph. As the system moves \nnortheastward, winds will shift to southerly for the coastal \nNorth Shore near and just offshore of the Grand Portage area, \nwhere gusts to 40 mph are possible again on Tuesday.\n\nExpect a third wind shift and even stronger westerly winds behind\na cold frontal passage on Wednesday morning. Gusts of 30 mph are\nforecast for land areas on Wednesday, with Lake Superior waters\nexceeding 40 mph. Gale Watches may be needed in time for the\ncoastal nearshore waters. After this wet and windy early to mid\nnext week time period, only very limited rain shower coverage is\nexpected to end next week.",
